        "day_number": 25,
        "title": "Chapter 7 The Manifestation of the Messiah (8)",
        "content": "<p>There are two concluding thoughts . First: The only hope of a decreasing self is an increasing Christ. There is too much of the self-life in us all, chafing against God's will, refusing God's gifts, instigating the very services we render to God, simulating humility and meekness for the praise of men. But how can we be rid of this accursed self-consciousness and pride? Ah! we must turn our back on our shadow, and our face towards Christ. We must look at all things from his standpoint, trying to realize always how they affect Him, and then entering into his emotions. It has been said that \"the woman who loves thinks with the brain of the man she loves\", and surely if we love Christ with a constraining passion, we shall think his thoughts and feel his joys, and no longer live to ourselves, but to Him.</p><p>\"Love took up the Harp of Life And smote on all its chords with might; Smote the chord of self, that trembling, Passed in music out of sight.\"</p><p>Second: we must view our relationship to Christ as the betrothal and marriage of our soul to our Maker and Redeemer, who is also our Husband. \"Therefore, my brethren,\" says the apostle, \"you also were made dead to the law through the body of Christ; that you should be married to another, even to Him who was raised from the dead, that we should bring forth fruit to God\" (Rom. 7. 4).</p><p>The Son of God is not content to love us. He cannot rest till He has all our love in return. \"He looks in at the windows\" of the soul, \"and shows Himself through the lattice.\" Our Beloved speaks, and says to us, \"Rise up, my love, my fair one, and come away.\" And, as our response, He waits to hear us say:</p><p>\"My Beloved is mine, and I am his; He feeds his flock among the lilies. Until the day break, and the shadows flee away, Turn, my Beloved!\"</p>",
