        "content": "<p><strong>Read:</strong> Isaiah 1:18; Psalm 51:7.</p><p><strong>Story context:</strong> After the prayer, Christie is relieved, but Treffy continues to struggle with doubt and guilt.</p><p><strong>Source-based editorial paraphrase:</strong> Christie meets Mabel and her mother, who listen to Treffy's plight. Mabel sends a fresh bunch of white snowdrops and teaches a short prayer about being made clean. Christie carries the flowers home as a tangible sign of care and a reminder of God's promise.</p><p><strong>Devotional focus:</strong> Feelings may lag behind the trust a person is learning to place in God. The flowers are not magic; their beauty helps the characters remember mercy, and their delivery is itself an act of human kindness.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> What concrete reminder of grace could help a worried person today? Offer encouragement together with practical support.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> God of mercy, steady those troubled by guilt or uncertainty. Let kindness and your promises draw them toward hope. Amen.</p>",
