        "content": "<p><strong>Read:</strong> Ephesians 2:8-10; Romans 5:1-2.</p><p><strong>Story context:</strong> Mr. Wilton visits Treffy and listens to his difficulty trusting that forgiveness could be freely given.</p><p><strong>Source-based editorial paraphrase:</strong> The clergyman uses an ordinary gift to show what it means to receive generosity rather than earn it. Treffy listens and prays with him and Christie. As the visitors part, the snowdrops continue to remind them of the mercy they have discussed.</p><p><strong>Devotional focus:</strong> Grace cannot be bought by merit or certainty of feeling. Faith receives God's gift in Christ and grows into gratitude and good works, not superiority over others.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> Where do you confuse receiving grace with proving yourself worthy? Thank God, then do one good work out of gratitude.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Generous God, teach me to receive your gift with humility and respond with love rather than fear. Amen.</p>",
