        "content": "<p><strong>Read:</strong> Psalm 34:18; Psalm 27:13-14.</p><p><strong>Story context:</strong> Christie's grief becomes especially acute after hearing the minister speak of his departed friend.</p><p><strong>Source-based editorial paraphrase:</strong> Christie tells Mr. Wilton that he wants to be with his mother and Treffy. The minister responds with tenderness and encourages him to continue living, serving, and trusting God. Christie returns to the attic still grieving, praying for patience.</p><p><strong>Devotional focus:</strong> A child's wish to join deceased loved ones is a signal for serious, compassionate attention—not a devotional ideal. Christie's need is for safety, ongoing adult care, and reasons to hope within his present life.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> If a grieving person says they want to die, take it seriously: stay with them and help them obtain immediate, qualified support. Do not rely on encouragement alone.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Lord of life, shelter those overwhelmed by grief. Equip us to notice danger, seek help, and offer steady love. Amen.</p>",
