    "description": "Twenty-seven Scripture-led devotional readings editorially adapted from Small Means and Great Ends, an 1851 anthology edited by M. H. Adams with contributions from several writers. Following the sequence of the inherited selections, these readings consider modest generosity, grief and care for children, fear and hope, truthful speech, human dignity across cultural divides, conscience, neighborly service, gratitude, and care for creation. The Scripture readings are newly curated thematic anchors; all reflections, devotional summaries, practices, and prayers are new editorial writing, not quotations from the anthology. Some nineteenth-century stories portray Indigenous people through prejudice, describe severe treatment of children, or draw overly certain conclusions about bereavement; those perspectives are identified as historical and are not endorsed. The original anthology remains accessible at the linked source for readers who wish to study its period context.",
