      {
        "day_number": 27,
        "title": "A Second Beginning Is Still a Beginning",
        "content": "<p><strong>Read:</strong> Lamentations 3:22-23; Galatians 6:1-2</p><p><strong>Source and historical context:</strong> Ranney speaks about alcohol’s harm to a man’s family, then turns to Jim, a skilled bricklayer separated from his wife and children. Jim comes forward at a mission but slips back into drinking before returning to seek help again.</p><p><strong>Devotional focus:</strong> A setback can be painful without proving that the person’s desire to change was false. The call to restore gently means resisting contempt and keeping pathways to support open. Relationships may need boundaries and professional treatment may be necessary. People deserve encouragement grounded in realistic care, not public pressure or promises that one attempt will solve everything.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> How can I support change without turning a setback into a verdict on someone’s character?</p><p><strong>Practice:</strong> Replace one judgmental phrase with a compassionate question. Help identify a next step, whether practical assistance, peer support, or professional care.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> God of new mercies, give us patience to begin again and love that does not shame the struggling. Amen.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Lamentations 3:22-23",
          "Galatians 6:1-2"
        ]
