      {
        "day_number": 28,
        "title": "Tools, Work, and Reconnection",
        "content": "<p><strong>Read:</strong> James 2:15-17; 1 John 3:16-18</p><p><strong>Source and historical context:</strong> When Jim sustains a period of sobriety, Dave helps recover his pawned work tools and find a bricklaying job. Jim saves money, reaches out to his wife, and the family eventually reconnects. The memoir presents these as important practical supports.</p><p><strong>Devotional focus:</strong> Hope becomes tangible through tools, work, honest communication, and stable relationships. Faithful care asks not only what someone believes but what they need to live safely and responsibly. Reconciliation with family must remain voluntary, especially when past conduct has caused harm. No one should have to return to an unsafe situation to validate another person’s recovery.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> What practical obstacle could I help remove without taking away another person’s choice?</p><p><strong>Practice:</strong> Consider donating tools, mentoring, supporting employment access, or helping someone connect with a legitimate service. Respect privacy and any family member’s boundaries.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> God, let our love take practical shape, and help every home seek safety and trust. Amen.</p>",
        "references": [
          "James 2:15-17",
          "1 John 3:16-18"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 29,
        "title": "See the Person Beyond the Label",
        "content": "<p><strong>Read:</strong> Isaiah 58:7-11; Luke 15:1-7</p><p><strong>Source and historical context:</strong> Ranney describes overcrowded Bowery lodging houses and people who had once held respected positions. At the Squirrel Inn he offers food and lodging assistance; he recounts helping a man living with anger over a long-ago injury, then meeting a young worker in distress.</p><p><strong>Devotional focus:</strong> A neighborhood’s reputation is never the full story of its residents. Homelessness is shaped by many circumstances, not by a single moral cause. Listening well means seeing the individual with a history, gifts, and needs. Forgiveness may help heal resentment, but it does not erase injustice or require renewed contact with someone who harmed us.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> Whose humanity have I hidden behind words like failure, addict, or outsider?</p><p><strong>Practice:</strong> Speak about someone facing hardship without reducing them to a label. Support material help alongside opportunities for their voice to be heard.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> God of steadfast love, teach me to recognize the dignity you give every neighbor. Amen.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Isaiah 58:7-11",
          "Luke 15:1-7"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 30,
        "title": "Go with Someone Who Is Afraid",
        "content": "<p><strong>Read:</strong> Luke 15:20-24; Ecclesiastes 4:9-10</p><p><strong>Source and historical context:</strong> A young man who left his job becomes stranded after spending his money. Ranney offers lodging and accompanies him home, where his family welcomes him. He then begins a longer account of another young man, Tom, drawn toward New York by promises of easy pleasure.</p><p><strong>Devotional focus:</strong> Sometimes the hardest part of returning is facing people we fear we disappointed. Companionship can make that first step possible, but it should be offered only when home is safe. Ranney’s story highlights the value of going alongside someone rather than merely giving instructions. When we welcome people back, patience and safety are more useful than a speech about their mistakes.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> Who may need accompaniment more than advice right now?</p><p><strong>Practice:</strong> Offer to accompany someone to a safe, meaningful appointment or conversation. Do not push them toward an unsafe home or relationship.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Father of compassion, give us courage to ask for help and grace to accompany one another. Amen.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Luke 15:20-24",
          "Ecclesiastes 4:9-10"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 31,
        "title": "A Return Shaped by Mercy",
        "content": "<p><strong>Read:</strong> Luke 15:17-24; Micah 6:8</p><p><strong>Source and historical context:</strong> The story of Tom continues: he loses money and housing in New York, visits a mission, and begins writing home with Ranney’s help. After months, Tom’s father provides money for a return. Ranney presents this as a prodigal-son story and later reports Tom rebuilding his life.</p><p><strong>Devotional focus:</strong> Jesus’ parable centers on a father who runs to embrace his child. The point is not that hunger and humiliation are useful punishments; no one should be deprived of basic care to teach a lesson. Honest responsibility and safe restoration can go together. A person returning from a hard season needs welcome, material support, and room to grow rather than endless reminders of failure.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> Am I withholding compassion until someone has suffered enough to deserve it?</p><p><strong>Practice:</strong> Offer help that meets immediate needs while also supporting long-term stability. If reconciliation is possible and safe, lead with listening and welcome.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Loving Father, free me from pride and teach me to welcome others with generous mercy. Amen.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Luke 15:17-24",
          "Micah 6:8"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 32,
        "title": "A Life That Keeps the Door Open",
        "content": "<p><strong>Read:</strong> Philippians 1:6; Hebrews 13:1-2</p><p><strong>Source and historical context:</strong> In his closing pages, Ranney encounters a police officer who remembers his arrests and recalls being treated suspiciously in Atlantic City. Later he returns there to speak publicly. He ends by inviting readers to visit him at the Squirrel Inn mission.</p><p><strong>Devotional focus:</strong> A person may carry the memory of an old label long after their life has changed. Our task is not to deny history but to let truth, accountability, and new conduct shape the present. Ranney closes not by claiming perfection but by keeping a door open to people who need welcome. The most convincing testimony is often a place at the table and a willingness to serve.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> What door of belonging could I open to someone others have written off?</p><p><strong>Practice:</strong> Reach out to a person who may feel excluded, and offer specific, respectful welcome. Keep that invitation free of pressure or demands for a dramatic story.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Lord, continue your good work in us, and make our homes and communities places of welcome. Amen.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Philippians 1:6",
          "Hebrews 13:1-2"
        ]
      }
