      {
        "day_number": 6,
        "title": "Use the Time You Have",
        "content": "<p><strong>Read:</strong> John 9:4; Ephesians 5:15-16.</p><p><strong>Story and historical context:</strong> The Modern Dorcas is written as a young woman's letter after the death of her sixteen-year-old friend Amelia. The story uses an early death to urge readers toward immediate service, a common pattern in nineteenth-century religious literature.</p><p><strong>Devotional focus:</strong> Life's uncertainty invites faithful attention to today. We need not live in fear or prove our worth through constant activity; grace frees us to do the good God places within reach.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> What loving action have you postponed for a more convenient season? Choose one realistic act you can complete today without neglecting rest or responsibility.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Eternal God, teach me to receive today as your gift. Lead me into the good work prepared for me, with freedom from fear and striving. Amen.</p>",
