        "title": "Repentance Begins to Change Habits",
        "content": "<p><strong>Read:</strong> Acts 3:19; Titus 2:11-12.</p><p><strong>Story and historical context:</strong> Mark reconsiders his ridicule of faith, chooses not to attend a harmful gathering, works willingly, and speaks more kindly at home. The tale portrays change beginning through small, observable choices.</p><p><strong>Devotional focus:</strong> Repentance is more than feeling ashamed. God's grace turns us toward him and trains us to replace destructive habits with truthful, loving, and responsible action.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> What small choice would show that your repentance is becoming practice? Make the action specific enough to complete and invite a trustworthy person to encourage you.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> God of grace, turn me from sin and train me in a new way of life. Let changed habits bear witness to your patient work. Amen.</p>",
