        "content": "<p>Morning</p>\n<p><em>“They took Jesus, and led him away.”</em></p>\n<p>John 19:16</p>\n<p><strong>Editorial morning reflection:</strong> This verse directs our attention to Jesus Christ, the eternal Son who became truly human for our salvation. Receive what it reveals about his character or work, and let trust in him take visible form in love.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Read the verse once more before the day gathers pace. Name one concrete way its truth can shape your attention, speech, or action today.</p>\n<p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Lord Jesus Christ, true God and true human, draw me into your life and teach me to follow you in love. Amen.</p>\n<p>Evening</p>\n<p><em>“All we like sheep have gone astray; we have turned every one to his own way; and the Lord has laid on him the iniquity of us all.”</em></p>\n<p>Isaiah 53:6</p>\n<p><strong>Editorial evening reflection:</strong> Scripture's call to repentance is an invitation to truth and restored life, not permission to shame or terrorize. Name actual wrongdoing, protect anyone harmed, receive Christ's mercy, and take a concrete step toward repair.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Read the verse once more and review the day without denial or self-contempt. Give thanks, name what needs repair, and entrust tomorrow to God.</p>\n<p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Holy and merciful God, give me courage to confess truthfully, receive forgiveness, and repair what I can. Amen.</p>",
        "references": [
          "John 19:16",
          "Isaiah 53:6"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 95,
        "title": "April 4",
        "content": "<p>Morning</p>\n<p><em>“For he has made him to be sin for us, who knew no sin; that we might be made the righteousness of God in him.”</em></p>\n<p>2 Corinthians 5:21</p>\n<p><strong>Editorial morning reflection:</strong> Scripture's call to repentance is an invitation to truth and restored life, not permission to shame or terrorize. Name actual wrongdoing, protect anyone harmed, receive Christ's mercy, and take a concrete step toward repair.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Read the verse once more before the day gathers pace. Name one concrete way its truth can shape your attention, speech, or action today.</p>\n<p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Holy and merciful God, give me courage to confess truthfully, receive forgiveness, and repair what I can. Amen.</p>\n<p>Evening</p>\n<p><em>“Come you, and let us go up to the mountain of the Lord.”</em></p>\n<p>Isaiah 2:3</p>\n<p><strong>Editorial evening reflection:</strong> Biblical hope is patient confidence in God's faithfulness, not a promise that every difficulty will quickly disappear. Let hope make room for honest lament and sustain one concrete act of love today.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Read the verse once more and review the day without denial or self-contempt. Give thanks, name what needs repair, and entrust tomorrow to God.</p>\n<p><strong>Prayer:</strong> God of hope, keep me faithful while I wait and let your promised future strengthen love in the present. Amen.</p>",
