    "days": [
      {
        "day_number": 1,
        "title": "A Light That Leads Us Home",
        "content": "<p><strong>Read:</strong> John 3:16-17; Romans 5:1</p><p><strong>Historical context:</strong> William Penn preached to a Quaker meeting in London in 1694. An 1836 publisher added a later preface to the surviving edition; this reading begins in Penn's sermon. He speaks of Christ as the light who makes peace with God possible.</p><p><strong>Source-based editorial paraphrase:</strong> Those who receive Christ's light and turn from sin may find peace with God.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> Penn invites hearers to receive the light of Christ and turn from what damages their communion with God. Jesus' gift comes before our effort to change. Where do you need his light today? Name one honest step toward peace with God and with another person.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Lord Jesus, thank you for coming toward us in love. Show me what I need to bring into your light, and lead me in the way of peace. Amen.</p>",
        "references": [
          "John 3:16-17",
          "Romans 5:1"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 2,
        "title": "Christ Is Near in Our Weakness",
        "content": "<p><strong>Read:</strong> John 14:6; Hebrews 4:15-16</p><p><strong>Historical context:</strong> Penn's sermon places reconciliation with God in Jesus Christ. Later in this section he also urges self-examination; his invitation begins with Christ's help.</p><p><strong>Source-based editorial paraphrase:</strong> Only Jesus Christ can bring people back into communion with God; he is the light and leader of his people.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> A truthful look at our lives can reveal weakness without leaving us alone with it. Hebrews says Jesus knows temptation and invites us to approach him for mercy. What need have you been hiding because you think you must solve it first? Bring it to him plainly.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Merciful Christ, meet me in weakness. Give me courage to be truthful, to receive your grace, and to take the next faithful step. Amen.</p>",
        "references": [
          "John 14:6",
          "Hebrews 4:15-16"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 3,
        "title": "Living to the One Who Loves Us",
        "content": "<p><strong>Read:</strong> Romans 6:11-14; 2 Corinthians 5:14-15</p><p><strong>Historical context:</strong> Penn turns from receiving Christ to the shape of a changed life. His language of dying to sin echoes Paul's account of being alive to God in Christ.</p><p><strong>Source-based editorial paraphrase:</strong> Penn urges believers to turn from sin and live for Christ, who died for them.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> Christ's love can change what we choose when an old habit calls. Change is usually practiced in ordinary decisions, with grace for the times we stumble. Which habit draws you away from love of God or neighbor? Ask for help with one concrete choice today.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Jesus, you gave yourself for us. Teach me to live in your grace, to turn from harmful habits, and to love the people before me today. Amen.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Romans 6:11-14",
          "2 Corinthians 5:14-15"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 4,
        "title": "Look to Jesus When Weary",
        "content": "<p><strong>Read:</strong> Hebrews 12:1-3</p><p><strong>Historical context:</strong> This part of Penn's sermon quotes Hebrews at length and addresses persecution. The source thought is summarized here so the Scripture itself can be read in its full setting.</p><p><strong>Source-based editorial paraphrase:</strong> Penn urges his hearers to keep Jesus in view through trials. Drawing on Hebrews 12, he points to Christ's endurance and calls weary believers to continue their journey with patience.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> Hebrews does not ask us to pretend that a hard road is easy. It points us to Jesus, who endured suffering and now sustains his people. What is making you weary? Consider a small next step, and let someone trustworthy know where you need support.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Patient Lord, keep my eyes on Jesus. Strengthen me in difficulty, and help me receive the companionship and care I need. Amen.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Hebrews 12:1-3"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 5,
        "title": "The Shepherd Who Seeks the Lost",
        "content": "<p><strong>Read:</strong> Luke 15:3-7; Isaiah 40:11</p><p><strong>Historical context:</strong> Penn ends his sermon with the image of Christ gathering wandering sheep. He draws together Jesus' lost-sheep teaching and the Bible's picture of a shepherd's care.</p><p><strong>Source-based editorial paraphrase:</strong> Penn pictures Christ as a shepherd who seeks wandering people, carries them, and leads them to rest and life.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> A wandering sheep needs to be found and carried, not scolded into finding the way alone. Jesus describes the shepherd's joy in finding what was lost. Where do you need to receive his care? Who near you might need patient, practical help today?</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Good Shepherd, seek those who feel far away, including me. Carry the weary, guide our steps, and teach us to care for one another. Amen.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Luke 15:3-7",
          "Isaiah 40:11"
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
