        "day_number": 3,
        "title": "Faith Expressed in Public Life",
        "content": "<p><strong>Read:</strong> Micah 6:8; Romans 13:10</p><p><strong>Historical context:</strong> Burt also argued that government should favor Christianity, a claim shaped by his 1855 setting. This selection concerns the believer's own conduct in public life, where faith can be shown through justice, mercy, and respect for every neighbor's conscience.</p><p><strong>Source-based editorial paraphrase:</strong> Burt argues that personal faith should shape a person's conduct in every relationship.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> Our choices as neighbors, workers, and citizens affect other people. Micah names justice, mercy, and humble walking with God. Where could you practice these today in a public responsibility, especially toward someone whose beliefs differ from yours?</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> God, shape my public and private choices with justice and mercy. Help me honor the conscience and dignity of every neighbor. Amen.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Micah 6:8",
          "Romans 13:10"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 4,
        "title": "A Family of Nations",
        "content": "<p><strong>Read:</strong> Acts 17:26-27; Philippians 2:3-4</p><p><strong>Historical context:</strong> Burt turns from one country's concerns to the bonds among nations. His image of a human family draws on Paul's speech in Acts 17.</p><p><strong>Source-based editorial paraphrase:</strong> Burt says nations depend on one another and owe care to one another. Love of country has limits within a wider human family.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> Love of home need not close our eyes to people elsewhere. Goods, labor, knowledge, and care cross borders every day. Think of one person or community beyond your own country whose well-being is connected to yours. How can you pray, learn, or act with care?</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Creator of every people, widen my concern beyond familiar borders. Help nations and neighbors work for one another's good. Amen.</p>",
        "references": [
