    "title": "Faithful Service and Hope in Christ: Eleven Readings from the Francis Storr Memorials",
    "description": "Eleven Scripture-led readings adapted from 1888 memorial notices and sermons for Francis Storr, including notes from his final sermon on John the Baptist. The sequence draws out quiet service, grace in weakness, prayer, the gospel, grief, witness, repentance, and costly faithfulness while identifying the local memorial setting and removing sectarian or speculative claims.",
    "total_days": 11,
    "tags": [
      "knowledge",
      "wisdom",
      "discipleship"
    ],
    "cover_image": "https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1495446815901-a7297e633e8d?auto=format&fit=crop&w=1200&q=80",
    "source_url": "https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/52768",
    "days": [
      {
        "day_number": 1,
        "title": "Quiet Acts of Love",
        "content": "<p><strong>Read:</strong> 1 John 3:18; Matthew 6:3-4.</p><p><strong>Historical context:</strong> The 1888 memorial notices describe Francis Storr's long ministry in Brenchley and the practical help he offered neighbors in need. The details belong to one Victorian parish, while the call to active love reaches beyond it.</p><p><strong>Source theme, in contemporary language:</strong> Love becomes visible through ordinary service: welcoming people, making work possible, easing another person's burden, and giving without seeking attention. Quiet kindness can reveal the generosity of Christ.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> Who near you needs practical care rather than a general promise of concern? Choose one useful act you can complete without expecting recognition.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Generous God, make my love truthful and active. Show me the needs I can meet, and teach me to serve quietly in the name of Jesus. Amen.</p>",
        "references": [
          "1 John 3:18",
          "Matthew 6:3-4"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 2,
        "title": "Grace in Changed Circumstances",
        "content": "<p><strong>Read:</strong> 2 Corinthians 12:9; Psalm 119:11.</p><p><strong>Historical context:</strong> Storr gradually lost his sight during the final decade of his life. The memorial writers admired his continued ministry, his reliance on other people, and his deep memory of Scripture.</p><p><strong>Source theme, in contemporary language:</strong> Loss changes what a person can do, but it does not remove dignity, calling, or the grace of God. Receiving help and storing Scripture in the heart can sustain faithful service through changed circumstances.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> What limitation or change are you resisting? Ask what faithful life can look like now, including the help you may need to receive from others.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> God of sufficient grace, meet me in weakness and change. Plant your word deeply in me, and help me receive and offer care with humility. Amen.</p>",
        "references": [
          "2 Corinthians 12:9",
          "Psalm 119:11"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 3,
        "title": "Answer the Call Before You",
        "content": "<p><strong>Read:</strong> Ephesians 2:10; Colossians 3:23-24.</p><p><strong>Historical context:</strong> The memorial traces Storr's moves between several parishes and recalls spiritual, charitable, and public-health work during those years. It presents vocation through the choices of a nineteenth-century pastor.</p><p><strong>Source theme, in contemporary language:</strong> God can use faithful work in changing places and roles. Calling is expressed through attention to the people before us, responsible labor, and willingness to enter a new task when love requires it.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> Which responsibility in your present place deserves fuller attention? Consider whether faithfulness means staying, changing course, asking for help, or beginning a neglected task.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Lord, guide my work and my decisions. Help me recognize the good you have prepared and serve wholeheartedly wherever you place me. Amen.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Ephesians 2:10",
          "Colossians 3:23-24"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 4,
        "title": "Presence, Prayer, and Patient Care",
        "content": "<p><strong>Read:</strong> Romans 12:12-13; James 5:16.</p><p><strong>Historical context:</strong> Canon Edward Hoare's funeral sermon remembers Storr visiting homes, teaching young people, supporting mission work, praying with others, and preaching in Brenchley. These are local memories rather than a model restricted to clergy.</p><p><strong>Source theme, in contemporary language:</strong> Christian care joins presence with prayer. Listening, visiting, teaching, sharing resources, and bringing needs before God are ways the whole church can strengthen people over time.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> Who would be helped by your steady presence? Choose a person to contact, a need to pray for, and one practical way to remain available.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> God of compassion, make me present to people who need care. Deepen my prayer, patience, and willingness to share. Amen.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Romans 12:12-13",
          "James 5:16"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 5,
        "title": "The Grace of God in Christ",
        "content": "<p><strong>Read:</strong> Romans 1:16; Titus 3:4-7.</p><p><strong>Historical context:</strong> Hoare identifies the gospel of grace in Jesus Christ as the center of Storr's preaching. The sermon uses nineteenth-century Evangelical formulas; this reading restates its central confession in shared Christian language.</p><p><strong>Source theme, in contemporary language:</strong> Salvation begins in God's mercy, is accomplished through Jesus Christ, and brings new life through the Holy Spirit. Grace frees believers from boasting and forms them for faithful living and hope.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> Are you treating God's acceptance as something you must earn, or grace as permission to remain unchanged? Receive mercy and ask where renewed life should become visible.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Father, thank you for saving mercy given through Jesus Christ and renewed in us by the Holy Spirit. Keep me humble, grateful, and ready for good work. Amen.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Romans 1:16",
          "Titus 3:4-7"
        ]
