        "title": "Encourage One Another in Grief",
        "content": "<p><strong>Read:</strong> 1 Thessalonians 4:13-14; Hebrews 10:24-25.</p><p><strong>Historical context:</strong> W. May's memorial sermon speaks to a congregation grieving a pastor who had served it for thirty-four years. Its strongest reusable counsel is to encourage one another and continue bearing witness to Christ.</p><p><strong>Source theme, in contemporary language:</strong> Christian grief is real, yet the death and resurrection of Jesus give hope. A grieving community can make room for tears while gathering, encouraging one another, and continuing works of love.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> Who is carrying grief that others may have stopped noticing? Offer presence without forcing an explanation, and remind the person through patient care that they are not alone.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Risen Lord Jesus, comfort those who mourn. Make us patient companions and strengthen our shared hope in your resurrection. Amen.</p>",
