        "content": "<p><strong>Read:</strong> Luke 3:10-14; John 1:29.</p><p><strong>Historical context:</strong> Storr's sermon presents John the Baptist as a direct and practical witness. John called different hearers to repent through generosity, honesty, restraint, and justice, and he directed them to Jesus, the Lamb of God.</p><p><strong>Source theme, in contemporary language:</strong> Repentance is more than regret. It changes how people use possessions, power, speech, and work, while forgiveness and new life remain centered on Jesus rather than moral achievement.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> What fruit would make your repentance concrete? Choose one repair, act of generosity, honest decision, or restraint that matches what you are asking God to change.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Lamb of God, have mercy on me and lead me into a changed life. Make my repentance honest and its fruit useful to others. Amen.</p>",
