    "days": [
      {
        "day_number": 1,
        "title": "A Higher Allegiance",
        "content": "<p><strong>Read:</strong> Acts 5:29; Romans 13:1-4.</p><p><strong>Historical context:</strong> Mark Hopkins preached this sermon before Massachusetts officials in January 1839. He began with the apostles' refusal to stop bearing witness to Jesus and considered the limits of human authority.</p><p><strong>Source theme, in contemporary language:</strong> Public authority can serve the common good, but no human command can replace God's authority or possess the conscience. Christian obedience should be lawful and peaceable while remaining faithful to God.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> Where do approval, fear, or institutional pressure compete with your obedience to God? Ask whether your response is both faithful and shaped by the character of Jesus.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Sovereign God, give me a clear conscience and a humble spirit. Help me respect rightful authority and obey you when faithfulness is costly. Amen.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Acts 5:29",
          "Romans 13:1-4"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 2,
        "title": "God Sees the Inner Life",
        "content": "<p><strong>Read:</strong> 1 Samuel 16:7; Matthew 22:37-39.</p><p><strong>Historical context:</strong> Hopkins contrasted what courts and laws can observe with the motives known to God. His civic address therefore turned from outward compliance to personal character.</p><p><strong>Source theme, in contemporary language:</strong> God cares about the thoughts, loves, purposes, and choices that shape action. A good appearance cannot replace a heart learning to love God and neighbor.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> Which motive beneath your recent actions needs God's light? Bring it into prayer without excuses, and choose one action that expresses honest love.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> God who sees the heart, search me and renew my motives. By your Holy Spirit, teach me to love you fully and my neighbor faithfully. Amen.</p>",
        "references": [
          "1 Samuel 16:7",
          "Matthew 22:37-39"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 3,
        "title": "Freedom for Faithful Living",
        "content": "<p><strong>Read:</strong> Galatians 5:13-14; Romans 8:1-2.</p><p><strong>Historical context:</strong> The sermon distinguished civic freedom from the deeper freedom of a life directed toward what is good. Its nineteenth-century political argument is reframed here as personal discipleship.</p><p><strong>Source theme, in contemporary language:</strong> Freedom is more than the absence of restraint. In Christ, freedom releases people from slavery to sin so that they may love, serve, and choose what gives life.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> Where have you confused freedom with getting your own way? Consider how Christ's freedom could help you serve someone rather than protect your preference.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Lord Jesus, thank you for setting us free from sin. Direct my freedom toward love, holiness, and joyful service. Amen.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Galatians 5:13-14",
          "Romans 8:1-2"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 4,
        "title": "Neighbor Love Builds Common Life",
        "content": "<p><strong>Read:</strong> Jeremiah 29:7; Romans 12:18.</p><p><strong>Historical context:</strong> Hopkins argued that public structures alone cannot create a healthy community. He stressed the daily influence of families, neighbors, teachers, congregations, and citizens.</p><p><strong>Source theme, in contemporary language:</strong> The common good grows through truthful speech, patient relationships, useful work, and concern for neighbors. Institutions matter, yet ordinary habits also shape whether a community becomes peaceful and just.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> What is one ordinary way you affect the climate of your home, church, workplace, or neighborhood? Choose a practice that makes peace and protects another person's good.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> God of peace, make me a trustworthy neighbor. Use my words, work, and relationships to strengthen the community around me. Amen.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Jeremiah 29:7",
          "Romans 12:18"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 5,
        "title": "Renewal Reaches the Heart",
        "content": "<p><strong>Read:</strong> Romans 12:2; Titus 3:1-2.</p><p><strong>Historical context:</strong> The 1839 sermon observed that laws can restrain harm but cannot by themselves create virtue. Hopkins called his hearers to moral renewal rather than confidence in political systems alone.</p><p><strong>Source theme, in contemporary language:</strong> Rules have a proper role, but lasting change also requires renewed minds, awakened consciences, and practiced goodness. Christians contribute through gentleness, integrity, teaching, and service.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> Where are you demanding change from a system while excusing the same fault in yourself? Begin with repentance and one constructive act within your reach.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Merciful God, renew my mind and conduct. Make me ready for good work, gentle toward others, and honest about the change I need. Amen.</p>",
        "references": [
