      {
        "day_number": 6,
        "title": "Courage Without Contempt",
        "content": "<p><strong>Read:</strong> Daniel 3:16-18; Acts 4:19-20.</p><p><strong>Historical context:</strong> Hopkins warned that power can try to control conscience and that religion itself can be misused to support oppression. He praised principled resistance rather than ambition or mob violence.</p><p><strong>Source theme, in contemporary language:</strong> Faithful conscience may require a calm refusal to do wrong. Christian courage does not need hatred, contempt, or domination; it bears truthful witness and accepts the cost of obedience.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> Is there a wrong you need to refuse or a truth you need to state? Plan words and actions that are clear, peaceful, and free from the desire to humiliate anyone.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> God of truth, give me courage without contempt. Keep me faithful to Jesus and guard my witness from fear, pride, and hostility. Amen.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Daniel 3:16-18",
          "Acts 4:19-20"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 7,
        "title": "Leadership That Resists Flattery",
        "content": "<p><strong>Read:</strong> Mark 10:42-45; Proverbs 29:25.</p><p><strong>Historical context:</strong> Addressing officeholders directly, Hopkins warned leaders against trading principle for popularity. His immediate audience was political, but the warning applies to every form of leadership.</p><p><strong>Source theme, in contemporary language:</strong> Leadership becomes unfaithful when approval matters more than truth and service. Jesus defines greatness through service, and responsible leaders use authority for the good of others.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> Whose approval most affects your decisions? If you lead at home, work, church, or in public life, identify one choice that should be guided by service rather than praise.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Servant King, free me from the fear of people. Teach me to use every responsibility with truth, courage, and care for those I serve. Amen.</p>",
