        "title": "Justice Beyond Expediency",
        "content": "<p><strong>Read:</strong> Isaiah 1:17; James 2:1-4.</p><p><strong>Historical context:</strong> The sermon urged leaders to measure policy by justice, benevolence, and moral responsibility rather than short-term advantage. Its examples belong to an 1839 American political setting.</p><p><strong>Source theme, in contemporary language:</strong> Convenience and success cannot turn injustice into faithfulness. God's concern for the vulnerable calls both leaders and neighbors to reject favoritism, correct oppression, and pursue what is right.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> Where might convenience be hiding another person's burden from you? Listen to those affected, examine your partiality, and choose a just response you can sustain.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Just and merciful God, expose my favoritism and excuses. Help me defend the vulnerable and pursue justice with patience, truth, and love. Amen.</p>",
