    "source_url": "https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/49145",
    "days": [
      {
        "day_number": 1,
        "title": "Faithfulness When Traditions Are Questioned",
        "content": "<p><strong>Read:</strong> Colossians 3:12-17; John 17:20-23</p><p><strong>Source and historical context:</strong> In a lecture delivered to the Brighton English Church Union on November 27, 1867, and printed in 1868, M. W. Mayow warned that proposals following a Royal Commission might endanger the Book of Common Prayer. He called the debate a crisis for the Church of England and linked it to hopes for Christian unity.</p><p><strong>Devotional focus:</strong> Communities sometimes face changes that unsettle familiar forms of worship. Paul urges believers to clothe themselves with love, gratitude, and peace. That does not make every inherited practice beyond examination; it asks us to conduct difficult conversations with patience and fidelity. Our deepest unity is not maintained by anxiety alone but by listening to Christ and one another.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> When a practice I value is questioned, do I respond first with suspicion or with an effort to understand?</p><p><strong>Practice:</strong> Pray for people on different sides of a church disagreement. Name one question you could ask respectfully before taking a position.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Lord Jesus, keep us faithful in what matters and gentle toward those who see questions differently. Amen.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Colossians 3:12-17",
          "John 17:20-23"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 2,
        "title": "What Is Essential, and to Whom?",
        "content": "<p><strong>Read:</strong> Romans 14:1-6; 1 Corinthians 10:31-33</p><p><strong>Source and historical context:</strong> Mayow discusses the Royal Commission’s first report of August 1867 concerning clerical vestments. The commissioners judged such clothing not essential yet offensive to many, and proposed restrictions. Mayow argued that this conclusion failed to consider why vestments mattered to worshippers who valued them.</p><p><strong>Devotional focus:</strong> A dispute can become sharper when everyone uses the same word differently. The question of what is essential to salvation differs from what is meaningful for communal worship. Paul calls Christians to honor God while considering one another’s consciences. The first response need not be to declare one party foolish; it may be to discover what a cherished symbol communicates and whom a choice affects.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> Am I calling something insignificant simply because it does not matter to me?</p><p><strong>Practice:</strong> Before criticizing an unfamiliar worship custom, ask a participant what it means. Listen without pretending agreement.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> God of wisdom, help me distinguish the heart of faith from the forms by which people seek to express it. Amen.</p>",
        "references": [
