          "1 Corinthians 10:31-33"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 3,
        "title": "The Cross as a Sign, Not a Substitute",
        "content": "<p><strong>Read:</strong> Galatians 6:14; Romans 6:3-4</p><p><strong>Source and historical context:</strong> Mayow turns to Canon 30 of the Church of England’s 1603 canons. It defended the sign of the cross after baptism, even while acknowledging that the sign itself was not necessary for the sacrament’s validity. He used this example to challenge the proposed restriction of vestments.</p><p><strong>Devotional focus:</strong> A visible sign may help us remember a profound promise without becoming the source of that promise. Christians differ in how they practice baptism and interpret sacramental signs. Paul points beyond a gesture to belonging to the crucified and risen Christ. When a tradition helps us attend to Jesus and love others, we may receive it with gratitude; when it distracts or wounds, we should examine how it is used.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> What visible practice helps me remember Christ, and do I ever mistake the reminder for the reality?</p><p><strong>Practice:</strong> Reflect on the meaning of a familiar cross or baptismal remembrance. Let it lead to one humble action today.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Crucified and risen Lord, make our signs honest reminders of your grace and our shared calling. Amen.</p>",
        "references": [
