      {
        "day_number": 4,
        "title": "When an Outward Gesture Has Meaning",
        "content": "<p><strong>Read:</strong> Matthew 28:18-20; 1 Corinthians 12:12-13</p><p><strong>Source and historical context:</strong> Continuing the baptism example, Mayow quotes Canon 30’s distinction between baptism itself and the later making of the sign of the cross. He argues that an outward ceremony can convey reverence without adding to the sacrament. He also cites the Savoy Conference to explain the authority of established rubrics.</p><p><strong>Devotional focus:</strong> Meaningful practices should serve what they signify. Baptism directs attention to Christ and the community that receives the baptized person. The exact role of ceremonies differs among Christian churches, so this Anglican historical argument should not be treated as a command for every believer. Whatever the form, the call to become a people of welcome, discipleship, and mutual care remains urgent.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> Do our congregational symbols make newcomers feel invited into Christ’s life or tested on unfamiliar customs?</p><p><strong>Practice:</strong> If you help lead worship, explain one tradition in plain words and make room for sincere questions.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Father, let our worship show your welcome and help us honor those who are learning. Amen.</p>",
