    "days": [
      {
        "day_number": 1,
        "title": "Belonging to the Lord",
        "content": "<p><strong>Scripture:</strong> Romans 14:7-9</p><p><strong>Historical context:</strong> James Slade preached this sermon in Bolton after the death of a fellow minister, William Thistlethwaite. He began with Paul's assurance that we belong to the Lord in life and in death.</p><p><strong>From James Slade&#x27;s sermon:</strong> The eternal Son of God stooped down from heaven to earth; the Word, which was in the beginning with God and was God, was made flesh; “humbled Himself and became obedient to death;” “bore our sins in His own body on the tree:” died and rose again, rose as the first fruits of a sleeping world.</p><p><strong>Reflection:</strong> Slade connects that belonging to Jesus' incarnation, death, and resurrection. Our hope rests in what Christ has done, not in a claim to control our future. Where are you trying to carry everything alone today? Name that concern before the risen Lord and ask how belonging to him can shape your next step.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Risen Lord Jesus, remind me that I belong to you. Turn my attention from self-reliance to your faithful love, and guide my choices today. Amen.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Romans 14:7-9"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 2,
        "title": "Serving Christ in Daily Life",
        "content": "<p><strong>Scripture:</strong> Colossians 3:23-24</p><p><strong>Historical context:</strong> Slade moves from hope beyond death to ordinary responsibilities. He does not ask readers to abandon their work, but to bring it under Christ's care.</p><p><strong>From James Slade&#x27;s sermon:</strong> Each of them still, while in the body, attentive to the duties of his own calling: no earthly business or occupation, belonging to their respective conditions, despised or neglected; but all “done heartily as to the Lord, and not as to men:” all earthly desires so regulated, all earthly plans so formed, all earthly objects so pursued, as not to interrupt, much less to stifle, the life of God in the soul.</p><p><strong>Reflection:</strong> Serving the Lord includes the work already before us: earning a living, caring for another person, and keeping a promise. The question is not whether a task looks important, but whether love and integrity guide it. Choose one ordinary duty today and do it attentively for Christ and for the person it serves.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Lord Jesus, help me serve you in the responsibilities of this day. Give me patience, honesty, and care for the people affected by my work. Amen.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Colossians 3:23-24"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 3,
        "title": "Trust When We Grieve",
        "content": "<p><strong>Scripture:</strong> Romans 14:8</p><p><strong>Historical context:</strong> Slade reports words spoken by William Thistlethwaite during a painful illness. They describe one person's prayer, not a standard every believer must meet while dying or grieving.</p><p><strong>From James Slade&#x27;s sermon:</strong> I wish you distinctly to understand how I am: I have no ecstasies, no rapturous flights, but a calm composure, a quiet resting, a peaceful waiting for the Lord: and I desire the Lord to deal with me as seems to Him good: to give me patience, to give me his grace that I may endure to the end; and to continue or remove me at His pleasure.</p><p><strong>Reflection:</strong> Thistlethwaite asked for patience and grace, without claiming an extraordinary feeling. Faith can also speak through fear, tears, and questions. If you are grieving, you do not need to manufacture calm for God. Tell him honestly what you need, and receive the care of others when it is offered.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Merciful Lord, meet me in grief and uncertainty. Give me grace for this day and people who will sit with me in love. Amen.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Romans 14:8"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 4,
        "title": "Known by the Lord",
        "content": "<p><strong>Scripture:</strong> 2 Timothy 2:19</p><p><strong>Historical context:</strong> In the original sermon, Slade addressed a congregation mourning its pastor. His closing words turn from that local loss to the Lord's knowledge of his people.</p><p><strong>From James Slade&#x27;s sermon:</strong> The world knows them not; the earthly shepherd himself may not have known them all: but “the Lord knows them that are His.”</p><p><strong>Reflection:</strong> Human communities remember imperfectly. Some acts of faithfulness go unseen, and even a caring pastor cannot know everyone. Slade points toward a steadier comfort: the Lord knows those who are his. If you feel overlooked, bring that ache to God. If someone else is easily overlooked, offer them your attention today.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Lord, thank you for knowing your people. Comfort those who feel forgotten, and teach me to notice and care for them. Amen.</p>",
        "references": [
          "2 Timothy 2:19"
        ]
      }
