    "days": [
      {
        "day_number": 1,
        "title": "The Spirit Brings Jesus to Mind",
        "content": "<p><strong>Read:</strong> John 14:25-27; 1 Corinthians 11:23-26</p><p><strong>Historical context:</strong> Percy C. Morris wrote a guide to a new London church chapel in 1909. Its architecture is the historical setting for these readings; the building itself is not the object of devotion.</p><p><strong>Source-based editorial paraphrase:</strong> Morris says the chapel was intended for Holy Communion and dedicated to the Holy Spirit, whom Jesus calls the Spirit of truth and the Comforter who brings his words to remembrance.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> The Spirit directs attention to Jesus—his teaching, his peace, and his self-giving love. When Christians share Communion, they remember the Lord who gave himself and proclaim his death until he comes. Which word or action of Jesus do you need to remember today?</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Holy Spirit, bring the words of Jesus to mind. Form his peace and self-giving love in us as we remember him. Amen.</p>",
        "references": [
          "John 14:25-27",
          "1 Corinthians 11:23-26"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 2,
        "title": "Led by Light and Truth",
        "content": "<p><strong>Read:</strong> Psalm 43:3; John 16:13-15</p><p><strong>Historical context:</strong> The guide celebrates particular universities and makes broad historical claims for them. This reading keeps the artwork's prayer for light while leaving those institutional claims in the source.</p><p><strong>Source-based editorial paraphrase:</strong> The chapel roof pictured the Holy Spirit as a dove sending out rays of light. Nearby university emblems and the prayer “Send out your light and your truth” connected learning with humble dependence on God.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> Learning can serve truth, but education and prestige do not make anyone infallible. Jesus says the Spirit glorifies him. Ask whether a belief leads you toward Christ's truth, humility, justice, and love—and remain willing to be corrected.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Spirit of truth, lead us out of pride and confusion. Fix our attention on Jesus and teach us to use knowledge for our neighbors' good. Amen.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Psalm 43:3",
          "John 16:13-15"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 3,
        "title": "Abide in the True Vine",
        "content": "<p><strong>Read:</strong> John 15:1-5; Hebrews 10:10-14</p><p><strong>Historical context:</strong> Morris explains Anglican Eucharistic symbols and devotional art. Christians differ about sacred images and sacramental language; the Scripture focus here is Christ himself and his once-for-all offering.</p><p><strong>Source-based editorial paraphrase:</strong> Carved vines pointed to Jesus, the true vine, while the reredos depicted his crucifixion as his one self-offering on the cross. The art joined continuing life in Christ with the saving work he has completed.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> Branches bear fruit by remaining in the vine. Christian growth begins in communion with Jesus, not in proving our worth. What practice helps you abide in him—Scripture, prayer, Communion, rest, confession, or loving service? Choose one faithful step.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Lord Jesus, true vine and crucified Savior, keep us in your love. Let the life you give bear patient and generous fruit in us. Amen.</p>",
        "references": [
          "John 15:1-5",
          "Hebrews 10:10-14"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 4,
        "title": "Fruit Shaped by the Spirit",
        "content": "<p><strong>Read:</strong> Galatians 5:22-25</p><p><strong>Historical context:</strong> This source section also explains medieval images of penance and several church traditions. Those details remain in the linked guide; this reading centers on Paul's plainly stated fruit of the Spirit.</p><p><strong>Source-based editorial paraphrase:</strong> The chapel windows represented the fruit of the Spirit through flowers, plants, and scenes from Scripture. Morris intended the beauty of the glass to point beyond itself to character formed by God.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> Love, joy, peace, patience, kindness, goodness, faithfulness, gentleness, and self-control grow over time. We do not need to display every fruit perfectly today. Which one does a relationship or decision especially need from you?</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Holy Spirit, grow the life of Jesus in us. Make our words and choices loving, truthful, patient, and gentle today. Amen.</p>",
        "references": [
