      {
        "day_number": 5,
        "title": "The Spirit Bears Witness to Jesus",
        "content": "<p><strong>Read:</strong> Luke 1:35; Matthew 3:16-17; Acts 1:8</p><p><strong>Historical context:</strong> The figures and symbols belong to a particular church's visual tradition. The biblical center is the triune action they were meant to recall: the Father sends, the Son comes among us, and the Spirit rests upon and bears witness to him.</p><p><strong>Source-based editorial paraphrase:</strong> The east window placed the Annunciation and Jesus' baptism together because both scenes speak of the Holy Spirit. Other images showed apostles and witnesses carrying the good news outward.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> The Spirit's work does not end in private inspiration. Acts says the Spirit gives power to bear witness to Jesus. Where can your witness take the form of a truthful word, a reconciled relationship, or practical care?</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Father, through your Holy Spirit keep us close to your Son. Give us courage and love to bear faithful witness to Jesus. Amen.</p>",
