    "title": "Conscience, Justice, and Peace: Six Readings from a 1710 Civic Sermon",
    "description": "Six Scripture-led readings adapted from Andrew Snape's 1710 Lord Mayor sermon. The devotional sequence draws lessons from Naboth, conscience, accountable power, and peacemaking while clearly setting aside the sermon's royalist polemic and its one-sided account of English history.",
    "total_days": 6,
    "tags": [
      "knowledge",
      "wisdom",
      "discipleship"
    ],
    "cover_image": "https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1513635269975-59663e0ac1ad?auto=format&fit=crop&w=1200&q=80",
    "source_url": "https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/48370",
    "days": [
      {
        "day_number": 1,
        "title": "Read Historical Parallels Carefully",
        "content": "<p><strong>Read:</strong> 1 Kings 21:1-16; 2 Timothy 2:15</p><p><strong>Historical context:</strong> Andrew Snape preached this sermon at St. Paul's Cathedral in 1710 on a royal commemoration. He compared King Charles I with Naboth and attacked the king's opponents. These readings do not adopt that political parallel.</p><p><strong>Source-based editorial paraphrase:</strong> Snape briefly warns that biblical events and later history should be compared carefully. He says no human merit or suffering should be treated as equal to that of the crucified Redeemer, even while making an extended comparison of his own.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> Scripture can illuminate public life, but it can also be recruited to make our preferred side look innocent and its opponents wicked. Read the whole biblical account, test historical claims, and keep Jesus—not a ruler, party, or nation—as the standard.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> God of truth, teach us to handle Scripture faithfully. Guard us from using your word to flatter power or excuse our side. Keep our eyes on Jesus. Amen.</p>",
        "references": [
          "1 Kings 21:1-16",
          "2 Timothy 2:15"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 2,
        "title": "Conscience Before Power",
        "content": "<p><strong>Read:</strong> 1 Kings 21:1-4; Acts 5:29</p><p><strong>Historical context:</strong> Snape reads Naboth's refusal through Israel's inheritance law, then uses it to defend Charles I. The devotional lesson here remains with Naboth and the limits that faith and justice place on powerful demands.</p><p><strong>Source-based editorial paraphrase:</strong> Naboth would not surrender his ancestral vineyard merely because a king wanted it. Snape also acknowledges that God alone judges the heart without error and that human judgments should be charitable and cautious.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> Authority does not turn an unjust demand into a just one. A faithful refusal can be costly, so conscience needs Scripture, wise counsel, honest facts, and concern for those affected. Where do you need courage to say no—or humility to reconsider a demand you have made?</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Lord, form our consciences in truth and love. Give courage to resist injustice and humility to examine our own use of power. Amen.</p>",
        "references": [
          "1 Kings 21:1-4",
          "Acts 5:29"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 3,
        "title": "Religion Cannot Sanctify Injustice",
        "content": "<p><strong>Read:</strong> 1 Kings 21:8-16; Isaiah 58:6-10</p><p><strong>Historical context:</strong> The sermon emphasizes that Jezebel's plot used a fast, an assembly, false charges, and perjured witnesses. Snape then maps those details onto a disputed English execution; that royalist argument is left in the source.</p><p><strong>Source-based editorial paraphrase:</strong> Naboth's killers surrounded injustice with religious ceremony. Public fasting and solemn language did not make false witness, corrupt judgment, and murder holy.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> Religious words cannot cleanse a cruel plan. Isaiah joins true worship to freeing the oppressed, sharing bread, and refusing accusation. When a cause invokes God, examine its methods: Does it tell the truth, protect the vulnerable, and allow honest accountability?</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Holy God, expose worship that hides injustice. Make our prayer truthful and our devotion active in mercy, fairness, and care for people at risk. Amen.</p>",
        "references": [
          "1 Kings 21:8-16",
          "Isaiah 58:6-10"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 4,
        "title": "Power Under the Way of Jesus",
        "content": "<p><strong>Read:</strong> Mark 10:42-45; Proverbs 15:22</p><p><strong>Historical context:</strong> Snape's fourth section gives a partisan account of conflict between Charles I and Parliament. It is not reliable as a stand-alone history. Its descriptions of pressure, advisers, and unchecked power can prompt a broader examination of leadership.</p><p><strong>Source-based editorial paraphrase:</strong> The sermon describes political actors competing for control, dismissing trusted counsel, and treating opposition as treachery. Snape applies those charges almost entirely to one side.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> Jesus rejects leadership that lords power over others and names service as greatness. Healthy authority welcomes truthful counsel and clear limits. In your own influence—at home, work, church, or community—whose voice is easy to dismiss, and how can you listen responsibly?</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Servant King, reshape our use of authority. Give leaders wise counsel, protect those affected by their choices, and teach us to serve rather than dominate. Amen.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Mark 10:42-45",
          "Proverbs 15:22"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 5,
        "title": "Every Leader Is Accountable",
        "content": "<p><strong>Read:</strong> Psalm 146:3-5; Micah 6:8</p><p><strong>Historical context:</strong> Even while defending Charles I, Snape concedes that the reign included legitimate grievances, unjust demands, and mistakes. He writes that princes are fallible human beings whose advisers can mislead them.</p><p><strong>Source-based editorial paraphrase:</strong> No ruler is beyond error. Snape's concession interrupts the sermon’s idealized royal portrait and reminds readers that public authority must answer to justice rather than personal loyalty.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> Scripture tells us not to place saving trust in rulers. We may respect an office, pray for leaders, challenge wrongdoing, and protect neighbors at the same time. Which public claim do you need to examine by the demands of justice, mercy, and humility?</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> God, judge all power with truth and mercy. Give leaders humility, give communities courage, and help us seek justice without making an idol of any person or institution. Amen.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Psalm 146:3-5",
          "Micah 6:8"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 6,
        "title": "Peace Without Falsehood",
        "content": "<p><strong>Read:</strong> Ephesians 4:1-6; James 3:17-18</p><p><strong>Historical context:</strong> Snape laments the factions that fed the English Civil War, although his sermon also repeats inflammatory claims about the opposing faction. Historical grief can teach peace only when we examine the rhetoric of our own side too.</p><p><strong>Source-based editorial paraphrase:</strong> The sermon warns that suspicion, hostile labels, and party formation can harden into destructive division. It closes by urging civic leaders toward religion and virtue, framed through the politics of its time.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> Christian unity is neither uniformity nor silence about harm. James describes heavenly wisdom as peace-loving, gentle, open to reason, and sincere. What label or suspicion keeps you from hearing someone truthfully? Seek one step toward peace that does not conceal injustice.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Prince of Peace, free us from contempt and dishonest unity. Make us gentle, open to reason, courageous about harm, and eager to preserve the Spirit's bond of peace. Amen.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Ephesians 4:1-6",
          "James 3:17-18"
        ]
      }
    ]
