    "days": [
      {
        "day_number": 1,
        "title": "Number Our Days",
        "content": "<p><strong>Read:</strong> Psalm 90:12; Numbers 23:10.</p><p><strong>Historical context:</strong> William Wigan preached in 1697 after the death of Lady Elizabeth Cutts. His opening uses the funeral occasion to remind the living that youth, status, health, and plans cannot make life secure.</p><p><strong>Source theme, in contemporary language:</strong> Awareness of mortality can teach wisdom. Preparing to die is not gloomy speculation; it means learning to live before God with faith, repentance, love, and readiness.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> If you received today as a limited gift, what would deserve more attention? What resentment, neglected duty, or delayed kindness should you address?</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Eternal God, teach me to number my days. Make me ready to meet you by helping me live faithfully and lovingly today. Amen.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Psalm 90:12",
          "Numbers 23:10"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 2,
        "title": "Practice the Life You Desire",
        "content": "<p><strong>Read:</strong> Micah 6:8; James 1:22.</p><p><strong>Historical context:</strong> Wigan reflects on Balaam's wish to share the end of the righteous while warning that a good wish without a changed path is insufficient.</p><p><strong>Source theme, in contemporary language:</strong> A faithful end cannot be separated from a life that hears and obeys God. Hope should take shape in justice, mercy, humility, and steady practice.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> Which Christian conviction do you admire but rarely practice? Choose one action that makes your desire visible today.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Lord, keep my faith from remaining only a wish. Teach me to do justice, love mercy, and walk humbly with you. Amen.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Micah 6:8",
          "James 1:22"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 3,
        "title": "Hope Has a Name",
        "content": "<p><strong>Read:</strong> John 11:25-26; 2 Corinthians 5:1.</p><p><strong>Historical context:</strong> The sermon surveys human arguments about death, then turns to the Christian hope announced in Scripture. This adaptation centers that hope directly on Jesus.</p><p><strong>Source theme, in contemporary language:</strong> Christian hope is more than a general belief that the soul continues. Jesus is the resurrection and the life, and believers entrust both life and death to him.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> What fear rises when you think about death or loss? Speak that fear plainly to Christ, and answer it with his promise of resurrection life.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Jesus Christ, resurrection and life, hold me when I am afraid. Keep my hope in you and comfort all who grieve. Amen.</p>",
        "references": [
          "John 11:25-26",
          "2 Corinthians 5:1"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 4,
        "title": "Comfort Rooted in God's Love",
        "content": "<p><strong>Read:</strong> Romans 8:38-39; Psalm 23:4.</p><p><strong>Historical context:</strong> Wigan contrasts fragile worldly comforts with the promises of Scripture. His sermon addresses a particular congregation, while its call to seek lasting comfort remains useful.</p><p><strong>Source theme, in contemporary language:</strong> Possessions, reputation, and even beloved companions cannot pass through death with us. God's presence and love in Christ remain trustworthy in the darkest valley.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> Which temporary support are you asking to carry more weight than it can bear? Receive it gratefully, but place your deepest confidence in God's lasting love.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> God of all comfort, walk with me through every dark valley. Root my heart in the love from which nothing can separate us. Amen.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Romans 8:38-39",
          "Psalm 23:4"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 5,
        "title": "Raised with Christ",
        "content": "<p><strong>Read:</strong> 1 Corinthians 15:20-23; Philippians 3:20-21.</p><p><strong>Historical context:</strong> The sermon speaks of the body resting in hope and points to Jesus as the firstfruits of the resurrection. Its older language is restated here as a clear Christian confession.</p><p><strong>Source theme, in contemporary language:</strong> Because Jesus truly rose from the dead, Christian hope includes the resurrection of the body. Death is an enemy, but it will not have the final word over those who belong to Christ.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> How does bodily resurrection change the way you regard aging, sickness, burial, or care for another person's body? Let hope lead to tenderness rather than denial.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Risen Lord Jesus, thank you that death will not have the final word. Sustain the sick, comfort the grieving, and keep us faithful in hope. Amen.</p>",
        "references": [
          "1 Corinthians 15:20-23",
          "Philippians 3:20-21"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 6,
        "title": "Faithfulness Is Not Measured by Age",
        "content": "<p><strong>Read:</strong> Ecclesiastes 12:1; 1 Timothy 4:12.</p><p><strong>Historical context:</strong> Lady Cutts died at about eighteen, and Wigan remembered the faith and service shown in her short life. This devotion keeps the lesson while avoiding idealized claims about her rank or gender.</p><p><strong>Source theme, in contemporary language:</strong> A life need not be long to bear faithful fruit. Young believers can serve through speech, conduct, love, faith, and purity, while every generation can learn from their example.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> Do you dismiss your own contribution because of age, inexperience, or limited time? What faithful action is possible with the life and opportunity you have now?</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Creator, receive every season of my life. Help me serve without waiting for a more impressive age, role, or opportunity. Amen.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Ecclesiastes 12:1",
          "1 Timothy 4:12"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 7,
        "title": "Grieve with Hope",
        "content": "<p><strong>Read:</strong> 1 Thessalonians 4:13-14; Romans 14:8.</p><p><strong>Historical context:</strong> The sermon closes by addressing Lady Cutts's family and congregation. It honors real sorrow while grounding Christian hope in the death and resurrection of Jesus.</p><p><strong>Source theme, in contemporary language:</strong> Christian grief is still grief, yet it is not without hope. Whether we live or die, we belong to the Lord, and those who die in Christ await the resurrection.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> Make room for grief without forcing quick explanations. What memory, loss, or person do you need to entrust to the Lord with both sorrow and hope?</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Lord of the living and the dead, receive our tears. Keep us in Christ, comfort those who mourn, and strengthen our hope in the resurrection. Amen.</p>",
        "references": [
